@@ -2,17 +2,46 @@
 
 Verify that a webhook really came from PayCo.
 
+`verify` must receive the **raw request body bytes**. The signature is an HMAC over the
+exact bytes PayCo sent, so anything that has already parsed and re-serialized the body
+(whitespace, key order, number formatting) will not match.
+
+## Express
+
+If you use a global `express.json()`, capture the raw body in its `verify` callback:
+
 ```js
+app.use(
+  express.json({
+    verify: (req, _res, buf) => {
+      req.rawBody = buf;
+    },
+  }),
+);
+
 const { verify } = require("@payco/webhooks");
 
 app.post("/webhooks/payco", (req, res) => {
+  const event = verify(req.rawBody, req.headers["payco-signature"], process.env.PAYCO_WEBHOOK_SECRET);
+  // handle event
+  res.sendStatus(200);
+});
+```
+
+Or parse only the webhook route as raw bytes:
+
+```js
+app.post("/webhooks/payco", express.raw({ type: "application/json" }), (req, res) => {
   const event = verify(req.body, req.headers["payco-signature"], process.env.PAYCO_WEBHOOK_SECRET);
   // handle event
   res.sendStatus(200);
 });
 ```
 
-`verify` throws `SignatureVerificationError` when the signature doesn't match or the
+`verify` accepts a string, `Buffer` or `Uint8Array`. Passing a parsed object throws
+`SignatureVerificationError` instead of silently re-serializing it.
+
+`verify` also throws `SignatureVerificationError` when the signature doesn't match or the
 timestamp is more than 5 minutes old.
 
